  "sourceRefs": [
    {
      "sourceId": "source:barrett-woudhuysen-remigius-clovis",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Transcription, English translation of EA 2 §§ 1–3, opening and conclusion. Uncertain political context, widows and orphans, ransoming captives, strangers, younger and older visitors."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:avitus-of-vienne-letter-46",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Letter 46, pp. 75–76, especially p. 75 from Igitur qui celeber est natalis and p. 76 on the liberated captives. Avitus acknowledges his absence from the ceremony."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:shanzer-dating-baptism-of-clovis",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 29–31, 47–49 and 55–56. The traditional 496 chronology, the proposed identification of Avitus’s captives and the argument for Christmas 508."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Book II, chapters 29–31. Clotilda, the vow against the Alamanni, Remigius of Reims and the framing of the baptism in terms of Constantine."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:saint-remi-reims-baptistery",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Entry Baptême: the baptistery identified in the 1995 excavations beneath the cathedral and its proposed association with Clovis."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-tours-histories-ii-37",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "II.37. Passage through Touraine, hay taken from a poor man, defeat and death of Alaric and the losses among men from the Auvergne."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:mathisen-campus-vogladensis-2012",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 43–47 and 56–61. Distance from Poitiers in Gregory’s narrative, Vouillé and Voulon among the proposed battle locations."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:clovis-letter-to-the-bishops-bk1",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "No. 1, pp. 1–2: opening list of protected people and church property; injunction on ring-sealed episcopal letters for captives taken under the king’s peace and the truth of petitions."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:cologne-clovis-bishops-bk1",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "BK 1, Date and Transmission. Boretius dates the royal letter 507–511; the manuscripts place it in the canonical tradition."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:wood-arians-catholics-and-vouille",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 139–141: Gregory’s story about taking hay in Touraine, compared with the orders described in the royal letter."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:gregory-histories-krusch-levison",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Book II.37, final sentence, and II.38. Gifts to Saint Martin, the documents from Anastasius, royal dress and public distribution of gold and silver in Tours."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:mathisen-clovis-anastasius-508",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 79–83 and 105–109. Account of the 508 visit in Histories II.37–38, ceremonial clothing, largesse and competing readings of the emperor’s honours."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:halfond-orleans-first-council",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Opening and attendance paragraphs: 10 July 511 conclusion, the king’s summons, thirty-two bishops and their seven provinces."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:halfond-vouille-orleans-conciliar-tradition",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 161–163, especially p. 163 and n. 60. Cyprian of Bordeaux and five further bishops attended both Agde in 506 and Orléans in 511; canon 5 and prisoners."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:szafranowski-orleans-er1039",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "ER 1039, canon 5, Latin and translation: proceeds of gifts and fields from the king for buildings, support of priests and the poor, or ransoming captives."
    }
  ],
